=== layout: subsection

# 1. The Biologics Control Act of 1902

=== layout: one-panel

# 1. Questions (1 of 2)

@body

- What event led to the first U.S. law regulating biological products such as vaccines and antitoxins?
- Who was Jim the Horse?
- How did the Biologics Control Act make sure that vaccines and other biologics were safe for public use?
- Why did the government decide to require licenses for facilities that made biological products?
- What role did the Hygienic Laboratory (later the NIH) play in enforcing the new rules?
- How did this law begin the idea that the federal government should inspect and approve medical products before use?

=== layout: one-panel

# 1. Questions (2 of 2)

@body

- How did the Biologics Control Act influence later drug and vaccine laws in the United States?

=== layout: one-panel

# 1. A Medicine Can Carry a Second Disease

@body

- In 1901, diphtheria antitoxin contaminated with tetanus killed children in St. Louis. The antitoxin came from a horse named Jim.
- The intended active ingredient was useful. The contaminated preparation was lethal.
- This is a manufacturing problem: the identity of the treatment alone cannot establish the safety of each lot.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/about-fda/fda-history/milestones-us-food-and-drug-law

=== layout: one-panel

# 1. License Producers, Inspect Production

@body

- The 1902 Act required federal licensing of establishments and biological products, with inspection and oversight of production.
- The Hygienic Laboratory helped test products and establish standards; this institution later became part of NIH.
- Biologics vary with their living source and manufacturing process. Traceability, testing, and control of contamination matter before a product reaches patients.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/about-fda/fda-history/milestones-us-food-and-drug-law

=== layout: one-panel

# 1. Follow One Lot Backward

@body

- A patient becomes ill after an antitoxin injection. Which lot did they receive? Which other patients received it?
- Can the producer identify the source animal, preparation date, tests, storage conditions, and distribution records?
- The modern connection is lot control: a vaccine or recombinant protein needs reproducible identity, purity, potency, and appropriate safety testing.
- Knowing the gene sequence does not prove that a vial contains the right, uncontaminated product.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/about-fda/fda-history/milestones-us-food-and-drug-law

