=== layout: subsection

# 2. The Pure Food and Drug Act of 1906

=== layout: one-panel

# 2. Questions

@body

- What public health problems led to the creation of the Pure Food and Drug Act?
- How did the law define misbranding and adulteration, and why were these ideas important for consumer safety?
- Who was Dr. Harvey Wiley, and how did his "Poison Squad" experiments help convince Congress to act?
- How did the 1906 Act change how medicines and foods were labeled and sold in the United States?
- Why was this law considered the foundation for the future Food and Drug Administration (FDA)?
- How did this act help build public trust in science-based health regulation?

=== layout: one-panel

# 2. What Is Actually in the Bottle?

@body

- Adulteration concerns a product's composition or quality. Misbranding concerns false or misleading labeling.
- The 1906 law attacked adulterated and misbranded foods and drugs moving through interstate commerce.
- A customer needs to know what they are buying. An honest label still does not prove that a treatment works.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/about-fda/fda-history/milestones-us-food-and-drug-law

=== layout: one-panel

# 2. The Poison Squad and Public Pressure

@body

- Harvey Wiley led investigations of food adulteration and experiments on preservatives using volunteers nicknamed the Poison Squad.
- Investigative journalism and public concern helped turn food and drug problems into a federal responsibility.
- The Bureau of Chemistry enforced the 1906 law. The agency acquired the FDA name in 1930; the modern organization did not appear fully formed in 1906.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/about-fda/fda-history/milestones-us-food-and-drug-law

=== layout: one-panel

# 2. Truthful Label, Useless Cure

@body

- Imagine a bottle accurately labeled as containing alcohol and an herbal extract, marketed as a cure for diabetes.
- Identifying the ingredients answers one question. Establishing safety and clinical benefit answers two different questions.
- The limits of labeling-based enforcement help explain why later laws added stronger premarket and efficacy requirements.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/about-fda/fda-history/milestones-us-food-and-drug-law

=== layout: big-image

@image

![2. A Public Campaign for Pure Food](assets/set6_sources/topic_02_023.jpg)

@caption

The 1906 campaign made adulteration and misleading labels visible to consumers. What can a truthful label establish, and what does it leave untested?

=== layout: subsection

# 3. The Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act of 1938

=== layout: one-panel

# 3. Questions

@body

- What tragic event led to Congress passing the 1938 FD&C Act?
- How did the FD&C Act expand the government's ability to test and approve drugs before they reached the public?
- What new powers did the FDA receive to inspect manufacturing plants and control drug labeling?
- How did the Act change the meaning of "safe for use" when applied to drugs and cosmetics?
- How did this law build the structure for modern drug approval systems still used today?
- How did the new requirements protect consumers from untested or mislabeled medical products?

=== layout: one-panel

# 3. The Solvent Was the Problem

@body

- In 1937, Elixir Sulfanilamide used diethylene glycol as a solvent. More than 100 people died.
- Sulfanilamide had antibacterial activity. That did not make the new liquid formulation safe.
- A product includes the active molecule, formulation, contaminants, dose, route, and conditions of use. Changing one can change the risk.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/about-fda/fda-history/milestones-us-food-and-drug-law

=== layout: one-panel

# 3. Safety Before the Sale

@body

- The 1938 FD&C Act required evidence of safety for new drugs before marketing and strengthened inspection and enforcement powers.
- It also extended federal oversight to cosmetics and therapeutic devices.
- These categories did not all acquire the same approval system. Ordinary cosmetics generally do not undergo FDA premarket approval, apart from applicable requirements such as color-additive approval.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/about-fda/fda-history/milestones-us-food-and-drug-law

=== layout: one-panel

# 3. Do Not Give 1938 Credit for 1962

@body

- The central 1938 advance was new-drug safety review. The 1962 amendments added the modern requirement for substantial evidence of effectiveness.
- Safe and effective are separate claims. A useless treatment can harm patients by delaying useful care.
- For biotechnology, ask both whether the proposed mechanism can work and whether the manufactured product produces a worthwhile clinical outcome.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/about-fda/fda-history/milestones-us-food-and-drug-law

=== layout: big-image

@image

![3. The Elixir Sulfanilamide Formulation](assets/set6_sources/topic_03_171.png)

@caption

The active drug and the liquid formulation were different safety questions. The solvent turned a useful antibacterial compound into a deadly preparation.

=== layout: subsection

# 4. FIFRA: Registration, Risk, and Bt Crops

=== layout: one-panel

# 4. Questions

@body

- Why did the U.S. first pass Federal Insecticide, Fungicide, and Rodenticide Act in 1947, and what problem was it meant to solve?
- How does FIFRA help the government protect the environment as well as public health?
- How did the 1972 revision change the law from simple registration to full safety testing under the EPA?
- Why was it important to move pesticide regulation from the USDA to the EPA?
- How did FIFRA set the legal groundwork for regulating genetically modified pest-resistant crops like Bt corn?
- What kind of data do manufacturers have to provide to show a pesticide or GM crop is safe?

=== layout: one-panel

# 4. From Labels to Risk Review

@body

- FIFRA began in 1947 and was substantially strengthened in 1972 after EPA was established in 1970.
- Pesticide registration evaluates proposed uses, exposure, and risks under the applicable legal standards.
- The label specifies legally enforceable conditions of use. A pesticide is not safe under every dose, route, or use just because a product is registered.

@citation

https://www.epa.gov/regulation-biotechnology-under-tsca-and-fifra/overview-plant-incorporated-protectants

=== layout: one-panel

# 4. The Plant Makes the Pesticide

@body

- Bt corn produces an insecticidal protein derived from Bacillus thuringiensis.
- EPA regulates the pesticidal substance and the genetic material needed to produce it as a plant-incorporated protectant.
- Relevant questions include effects on target pests, non-target organisms, food-related exposures, and the evolution of resistant insects.
- The entire plant is not simply treated as a bottle of sprayed pesticide.

@citation

https://www.epa.gov/regulation-biotechnology-under-tsca-and-fifra/overview-plant-incorporated-protectants

=== layout: one-panel

# 4. Hazard, Exposure, and Resistance

@body

- Hazard asks what harm an agent can cause. Risk also depends on exposure and conditions of use.
- Do not confuse Bt insect resistance with herbicide tolerance. These are different traits with different biological consequences.
- Resistance management can require refuges and monitoring. A successful insecticidal trait creates selection pressure for insects that survive it.
- Ask what evidence would change the proposed use, restrictions, or monitoring plan.

@citation

https://www.epa.gov/regulation-biotechnology-under-tsca-and-fifra/overview-plant-incorporated-protectants

=== layout: big-image

@image

![4. Bt Corn: the Pesticidal Trait](assets/set6_drawings/bt_crop.drawn.svg)

@caption

The trait, exposure, target susceptibility, and selection for resistance connect the molecular mechanism to regulatory questions.

=== layout: one-panel

# 4. Glyphosate: Hazard and Exposure

@body

- The insecticidal Bt trait and herbicide tolerance are different examples. Herbicide exposure raises its own questions.
- IARC classified glyphosate as probably carcinogenic to humans in 2015, a hazard classification.
- Risk assessments also consider exposure, formulation, route, duration, and use. Different bodies can answer different questions or weigh evidence differently.
- The word WHO does not identify one interchangeable conclusion: IARC and the joint FAO/WHO pesticide-residue assessment have different roles.

@citation

https://www.iarc.who.int/wp-content/uploads/2018/07/MonographVolume112-1.pdf

=== layout: one-panel

# 4. Ask Which Exposure Was Studied

@body

{table-style=comparison}
| Scenario | What You Need to Know |
| --- | --- |
| An occupational applicator | Product, frequency, protective measures, and cumulative exposure |
| A dietary residue | Chemical, concentration, consumption, and the exposure estimate |
| An animal experiment | Dose, route, duration, controls, and relevance to people |

An old news headline or a breakfast-cereal serving calculation cannot replace this comparison.

@citation

https://www.iarc.who.int/wp-content/uploads/2018/07/MonographVolume112-1.pdf

